# Example 08:

# Finally, let's explore the nivolumab result, starting from the two proteins that interact directly with the drug and
# the disease:

match
let $dr, $p1 in drug_protein_interactions();
let $di, $p3 in disease_protein_interactions();
let $p1, $p2 in protein_associations();
let $p2, $p3 in protein_associations();
$di has name "Neoplasms";
$dr has dgidb-name "NIVOLUMAB";
$p1 has uniprot-id $drug-protein;
$p2 has uniprot-id $linking-protein;
$p3 has uniprot-id $disease-protein;
fetch { "drug-protein": $drug-protein, "linking-protein": $linking-protein, "disease-protein": $disease-protein };

# We see something different to cyclophosphamide in Example 07. Rather than directly interacting with the same protein,
# neoplasms and nivolumab interact with separate proteins: O00763 and O14497. Both of those are associated with a single
# common protein, O00231, via the protein_associations function we saw in Example 05.
#
# The three proteins are associated with each other via participation in shared metabolic pathways. This explains how
# the treatment might work: the drug affects a protein, which has an impact on a pathway, which has an impact on the
# expression of a second protein, which has an impact on a second pathway, which has an impact on a third protein, which
# finally impacts the spread of the cancer.
#
# Once again we can dig even deeper into the data, but this is sufficient to understand the treatment. TypeDB has
# revealed an extremely complex set of interactions between different biomolecules, which might lead to the discovery of
# a new treatment for neoplasms. We didn't specify any of this: composing the functions we defined pieced together the
# connections. Not only that, the derivations for cyclophosphamide and nivolumab are completely different, but our query
# for neoplasm treatments in Example 06 gave us both results by describing what we were looking for at a high level.

